  "original_full_abstract": "For two polytopes, retain the faces of their Minkowski sum whose two canonical summands have positive dimensions and whose dimension is the sum of those dimensions. This is the definition in Payne’s printed Oberwolfach question. Let D be the standard four-simplex and I = [0,1]. For the full-dimensional lattice polytopes D × I and (−D) × I in ℝ⁵, the qualifying-face order complex is a disjoint union of two connected pure graphs, each with 50 vertices and 60 edges. Its rational Betti numbers are (2,22,0,…). Thus both printed assertions, shellability and the homology of a wedge of three-spheres, fail. The shared interval factor deliberately produces a nongeneric example. The argument concerns the printed additive-dimension definition; it does not settle shellability for the different mixed faces used in stable tropical intersections.",
  "original_publication_date": "2026-10-17",
  "native_version_field_present": true,
  "native_version": "1.0.0",
  "printed_version_field_present": true,
  "printed_version": "1.0.0",
  "full_original_result": "Both full-dimensional lattice5-prisms D4×I and (-D4)×I; canonical positive/exact-dimension-additivity filter; ordinary strict-chain order complex with no added extrema is K disjoint union K, each connected pure50vertex60edge graph a wedge of11circles; ordinary rational Betti(2,22) and zero higher groups; both pure/nonpure nonshellability and printed3sphere-wedge target fail.",
  "limits_and_classical_credit_preserved": "Deliberate nongeneric shared interval; printed Problem3 has no general-position condition. Stable subset lower bounds, facet-based terminology and HSWWv2(C*)8 object remain distinct. Classical opposite tetrahedra and general Minkowski decomposition credited. No generic perturbation/minimality/firstness/worldwide openness/final-edition equivalence claim.",
